#Rigid-group definitions for the springs-only rotamer/hinge mechanism: all DOF
#together (backbone phi/psi, side-chain chi1..chi4, hydroxyl/thiol rotation).
#
#Same columnar syntax as the coarse-grain reduce rules (name resname atom1
#atom2 ...), reusing the same reader, but the ".rbody" extension marks that
#the two are read differently: a .grp line reduces its atoms into one output
#grain, a .rbody line instead creates every pairwise spring between its atoms
#and leaves the particles distinct -- nothing is reduced or merged.
#Adjacent groups overlap on exactly the 2 atoms of a hinge axis -- that overlap
#alone gives "2 anchors, 1 free rotation", nothing else needed.
#
#CHARMM-style +/- prefix: "+X"/"-X" means atom X of the next/previous residue
#in the chain, instead of the current one. A rule's resname always refers to
#its own (unprefixed) residue, matched regardless of what +/- neighbour it is.
#
#Naming <ResidueCode>_<Part>: _CA = CA tetrahedron. _PHI = peptide plane behind
#this residue {-CA, -C, -O, N, H, CA} (overlaps _CA on {N, CA}: the phi axis).
#_PSI = peptide plane ahead of this residue {CA, C, O, +N, +H, +CA} (overlaps
#_CA on {CA, C}: the psi axis). _PHI(i) and _PSI(i-1) are the same 6 atoms,
#deliberately written twice (once from each residue) so both planes around a
#given CA are visible together, for readability. First/last residue in a chain
#is missing -CA/-C/-O or +N/+H/+CA respectively: skip rather than fail. Same
#for +H when the next residue is proline (no amide H).
#_CB.._SD = chi1..4 pivots down the side chain. _RING = rigid aromatic/
#imidazole terminus.
#
#Arg's guanidinium is split into one group per real sp2 centre (_CZ, _NH1,
#_NH2), each holding that centre plus its 3 substituents, instead of one rigid
#clique. Adjacent groups overlap on exactly the 2 atoms of a C-N bond, so all
#three guanidinium C-N rotations become free hinges like every chi pivot, and
#amber99sb.xml's real torsion for each of them (generic X-CA-N2-X, n=2,
#phase=180deg, k=10.0416 kJ/mol, 4 substituent pairs per bond) governs them
#instead of the uniform --stiffness value. Splitting loses NO planarity:
#verified that all 4 of AMBER's guanidinium impropers (hubs NE/CZ/NH1/NH2,
#k=4.184/43.932/4.184/4.184 kJ/mol) have all 4 of their own atoms inside a
#single one of these groups, so each sp2 centre stays geometrically enforced
#by the rigid mesh -- only the relative twist BETWEEN centres is freed, which
#is exactly what AMBER parametrizes. NE-CZ (chi5) was already free before this
#split (_NE and the old _GUAN overlapped on exactly {NE,CZ}); this frees
#CZ-NH1 and CZ-NH2 as well.
#
#Aromatic rings (Phe/Tyr/His/Trp) are split into per-vertex groups too
#({the vertex, its 2 ring neighbours, its own H or exocyclic substituent}),
#NOT kept as one rigid clique. Unlike Pro's 5-ring (where bonds+angles alone
#hold every ring distance), a 6-ring's para pairs are 1-4: the ring can fold
#("boat") without changing any angle, and an sp2 H can leave the plane at
#first order -- so freeing these rings is only valid together with BOTH the
#ring proper torsions (X-CA-CA-X n=2 k=15.167, per-pair rings) AND the
#PLANARITY improper family (hub impropers k=4.60/4.18) in
#ProteinAtomBonded.bi.ff, which is exactly what AMBER itself uses. Each
#per-vertex group still holds its own vertex's improper geometry (all 4
#atoms of the local hub improper sit inside one group), so local planarity
#never regresses even in the -rigidbody-only base model; the freed modes
#are the ring torsions and the global fold, governed by the new springs.
#His lists both HD1 and HE2 (protonation-dependent, one is always absent --
#the builder's missing-atom tolerance already handled exactly this in the
#old H_RING line). Trp's junction carbons CD2/CE2 carry no AMBER improper
#(verified on a real built system) -- their planarity comes from the two
#rings' own torsions, matching AMBER.
#
#Asn/Gln's side-chain amides are split the same way and for the same reason
#(N_CG/N_ND2, Q_CD/Q_NE2, overlapping on exactly the C-N amide bond): the
#amide C-N rotation carries the same real AMBER torsion as the backbone
#peptide bond itself (4 pairs at n=2, k=10.46, phase=180deg, plus n=1 k=8.37
#on the (O,H) pairs -- verified on a real built system), and both AMBER
#impropers (hub C k=43.93, hub N k=4.18) keep all 4 of their atoms inside a
#single split group, so each sp2 centre stays geometrically planar; only the
#relative twist across the C-N bond is freed, governed by the new per-pair
#amide rings in ProteinAtomBonded.bi.ff.
#
#Requires the matching per-pair guanidinium rings in ProteinAtomBonded.bi.ff:
#a single combined ring per axis is NOT adequate here (measured x3.4 error on
#CZ-NH1 and an outright sign flip on CZ-NH2), because all 4 substituent pairs
#of these axes reinforce in phase and a combined ring therefore assumes
#maximal coherence -- see generate_sidechain_axis's per_pair flag. _SG/_OG/_OG1/_OH = hydroxyl/thiol
#pivot, {axis atom1, axis atom2, H} only -- the heavy atom stays in its parent
#group, only the H rotates.
#
#_NTER/_CTER: only needed for the chain's actual first/last residue, where
#_PHI/_PSI don't form (no -/+ neighbour). NTER's N carries NH3+ (H1/H2/H3)
#instead of the single amide H used everywhere else; CTER's C carries COO-
#(O, OXT) instead of the single carbonyl O -- both left unconstrained
#otherwise, so these two groups are not optional for a terminal residue.
#
#Assumes all-atom + H (PDB v3 naming). Pro's pyrrolidine is NOT one rigid
#clique: real prolines pucker (Cgamma-endo/exo, few-kJ/mol barriers, ps-ns
#timescales, coupled to phi), and the real molecule has no improper there --
#so the ring is held like every side CHAIN in this file, one group per
#vertex (P_CA the standard CA tetrahedron, P_CB/P_CG/P_CD each {parent,
#self, next, own H's}) plus P_NRING {CD N CA}, the ring-closure angle at N.
#In a 5-ring every atom pair is 1-2 or 1-3, so these groups alone hold
#every ring distance at bond/angle level; the pucker survives as the soft
#collective mode it really is (angle springs flex slightly, exactly as in a
#real force field), and the 4 ring torsions are free hinges governed by
#PRO's ring_* DIHEDRAL entries in ProteinAtomBonded.bi.ff (N-CD's torsion
#is owned by the single real pair (-C, CG) -- proline-specific C-N-CT-CT
#AMBER entries, all other pairs are genuine zeros). As NTER, Pro's ring
#nitrogen keeps CD and gets only 2 extra H (H2, H3), not 3. Terminal methyls (Ala CB,
#Ile/Thr/Val branch) included for completeness though not a real chi.

A_CA ALA N CA C CB HA
A_NTER ALA N CA H1 H2 H3
A_CTER ALA CA C O OXT
A_PHI ALA -CA -C -O N H CA
A_PSI ALA CA C O +N +H +CA
A_CB ALA CA CB HB1 HB2 HB3

R_CA ARG N CA C CB HA
R_NTER ARG N CA H1 H2 H3
R_CTER ARG CA C O OXT
R_PHI ARG -CA -C -O N H CA
R_PSI ARG CA C O +N +H +CA
R_CB ARG CA CB CG HB2 HB3
R_CG ARG CB CG CD HG2 HG3
R_CD ARG CG CD NE HD2 HD3
R_NE ARG CD NE CZ HE
R_CZ ARG NE CZ NH1 NH2
R_NH1 ARG CZ NH1 HH11 HH12
R_NH2 ARG CZ NH2 HH21 HH22

N_CA ASN N CA C CB HA
N_NTER ASN N CA H1 H2 H3
N_CTER ASN CA C O OXT
N_PHI ASN -CA -C -O N H CA
N_PSI ASN CA C O +N +H +CA
N_CB ASN CA CB CG HB2 HB3
N_CG ASN CB CG OD1 ND2
N_ND2 ASN CG ND2 HD21 HD22

D_CA ASP N CA C CB HA
D_NTER ASP N CA H1 H2 H3
D_CTER ASP CA C O OXT
D_PHI ASP -CA -C -O N H CA
D_PSI ASP CA C O +N +H +CA
D_CB ASP CA CB CG HB2 HB3
D_CG ASP CB CG OD1 OD2

C_CA CYS N CA C CB HA
C_NTER CYS N CA H1 H2 H3
C_CTER CYS CA C O OXT
C_PHI CYS -CA -C -O N H CA
C_PSI CYS CA C O +N +H +CA
C_CB CYS CA CB SG HB2 HB3
C_SG CYS CB SG HG

Q_CA GLN N CA C CB HA
Q_NTER GLN N CA H1 H2 H3
Q_CTER GLN CA C O OXT
Q_PHI GLN -CA -C -O N H CA
Q_PSI GLN CA C O +N +H +CA
Q_CB GLN CA CB CG HB2 HB3
Q_CG GLN CB CG CD HG2 HG3
Q_CD GLN CG CD OE1 NE2
Q_NE2 GLN CD NE2 HE21 HE22

E_CA GLU N CA C CB HA
E_NTER GLU N CA H1 H2 H3
E_CTER GLU CA C O OXT
E_PHI GLU -CA -C -O N H CA
E_PSI GLU CA C O +N +H +CA
E_CB GLU CA CB CG HB2 HB3
E_CG GLU CB CG CD HG2 HG3
E_CD GLU CG CD OE1 OE2

G_CA GLY N CA C HA2 HA3
G_NTER GLY N CA H1 H2 H3
G_CTER GLY CA C O OXT
G_PHI GLY -CA -C -O N H CA
G_PSI GLY CA C O +N +H +CA

H_CA HIS N CA C CB HA
H_NTER HIS N CA H1 H2 H3
H_CTER HIS CA C O OXT
H_PHI HIS -CA -C -O N H CA
H_PSI HIS CA C O +N +H +CA
H_CB HIS CA CB CG HB2 HB3
H_CG HIS CB CG ND1 CD2
H_ND1 HIS CG ND1 CE1 HD1
H_CE1 HIS ND1 CE1 NE2 HE1
H_NE2 HIS CE1 NE2 CD2 HE2
H_CD2 HIS CG CD2 NE2 HD2

I_CA ILE N CA C CB HA
I_NTER ILE N CA H1 H2 H3
I_CTER ILE CA C O OXT
I_PHI ILE -CA -C -O N H CA
I_PSI ILE CA C O +N +H +CA
I_CB ILE CA CB CG1 CG2 HB
I_CG1 ILE CB CG1 CD1 HG12 HG13
I_CG2 ILE CB CG2 HG21 HG22 HG23
I_CD1 ILE CG1 CD1 HD11 HD12 HD13

L_CA LEU N CA C CB HA
L_NTER LEU N CA H1 H2 H3
L_CTER LEU CA C O OXT
L_PHI LEU -CA -C -O N H CA
L_PSI LEU CA C O +N +H +CA
L_CB LEU CA CB CG HB2 HB3
L_CG LEU CB CG CD1 CD2 HG
L_CD1 LEU CG CD1 HD11 HD12 HD13
L_CD2 LEU CG CD2 HD21 HD22 HD23

K_CA LYS N CA C CB HA
K_NTER LYS N CA H1 H2 H3
K_CTER LYS CA C O OXT
K_PHI LYS -CA -C -O N H CA
K_PSI LYS CA C O +N +H +CA
K_CB LYS CA CB CG HB2 HB3
K_CG LYS CB CG CD HG2 HG3
K_CD LYS CG CD CE HD2 HD3
K_CE LYS CD CE NZ HE2 HE3
K_NZ LYS CE NZ HZ1 HZ2 HZ3

M_CA MET N CA C CB HA
M_NTER MET N CA H1 H2 H3
M_CTER MET CA C O OXT
M_PHI MET -CA -C -O N H CA
M_PSI MET CA C O +N +H +CA
M_CB MET CA CB CG HB2 HB3
M_CG MET CB CG SD HG2 HG3
M_SD MET CG SD CE
M_CE MET SD CE HE1 HE2 HE3

F_CA PHE N CA C CB HA
F_NTER PHE N CA H1 H2 H3
F_CTER PHE CA C O OXT
F_PHI PHE -CA -C -O N H CA
F_PSI PHE CA C O +N +H +CA
F_CB PHE CA CB CG HB2 HB3
F_CG PHE CB CG CD1 CD2
F_CD1 PHE CG CD1 CE1 HD1
F_CD2 PHE CG CD2 CE2 HD2
F_CE1 PHE CD1 CE1 CZ HE1
F_CE2 PHE CD2 CE2 CZ HE2
F_CZ PHE CE1 CZ CE2 HZ

#Note: P_PHI/P_RING deliberately never connect "-C" to CD (the real
#(-C)-N-CD angle, from the ring closing onto N instead of an amide H, is
#the actual physical restriction on Pro's phi). It's added by
#--bondedinteraction alone (BondedForceFieldReader's fallback spring, with
#the real AMBER angle constant), not here: a uniform --stiffness spring
#with no real angle constant would freeze phi completely instead of
#realistically restricting it -- see IO/BondedForceFieldReader.h.
P_CA PRO N CA C CB HA
P_CB PRO CA CB CG HB2 HB3
P_CG PRO CB CG CD HG2 HG3
P_CD PRO CG CD N HD2 HD3
P_NRING PRO CD N CA
P_NTER PRO N CA CD H2 H3
P_CTER PRO CA C O OXT
P_PHI PRO -CA -C -O N CA
P_PSI PRO CA C O +N +H +CA

S_CA SER N CA C CB HA
S_NTER SER N CA H1 H2 H3
S_CTER SER CA C O OXT
S_PHI SER -CA -C -O N H CA
S_PSI SER CA C O +N +H +CA
S_CB SER CA CB OG HB2 HB3
S_OG SER CB OG HG

T_CA THR N CA C CB HA
T_NTER THR N CA H1 H2 H3
T_CTER THR CA C O OXT
T_PHI THR -CA -C -O N H CA
T_PSI THR CA C O +N +H +CA
T_CB THR CA CB OG1 CG2 HB
T_OG1 THR CB OG1 HG1
T_CG2 THR CB CG2 HG21 HG22 HG23

W_CA TRP N CA C CB HA
W_NTER TRP N CA H1 H2 H3
W_CTER TRP CA C O OXT
W_PHI TRP -CA -C -O N H CA
W_PSI TRP CA C O +N +H +CA
W_CB TRP CA CB CG HB2 HB3
W_CG TRP CB CG CD1 CD2
W_CD1 TRP CG CD1 NE1 HD1
W_NE1 TRP CD1 NE1 CE2 HE1
W_CE2 TRP NE1 CE2 CD2 CZ2
W_CD2 TRP CG CD2 CE2 CE3
W_CE3 TRP CD2 CE3 CZ3 HE3
W_CZ3 TRP CE3 CZ3 CH2 HZ3
W_CH2 TRP CZ3 CH2 CZ2 HH2
W_CZ2 TRP CH2 CZ2 CE2 HZ2

Y_CA TYR N CA C CB HA
Y_NTER TYR N CA H1 H2 H3
Y_CTER TYR CA C O OXT
Y_PHI TYR -CA -C -O N H CA
Y_PSI TYR CA C O +N +H +CA
Y_CB TYR CA CB CG HB2 HB3
Y_CG TYR CB CG CD1 CD2
Y_CD1 TYR CG CD1 CE1 HD1
Y_CD2 TYR CG CD2 CE2 HD2
Y_CE1 TYR CD1 CE1 CZ HE1
Y_CE2 TYR CD2 CE2 CZ HE2
Y_CZ TYR CE1 CZ CE2 OH
Y_OH TYR CZ OH HH

V_CA VAL N CA C CB HA
V_NTER VAL N CA H1 H2 H3
V_CTER VAL CA C O OXT
V_PHI VAL -CA -C -O N H CA
V_PSI VAL CA C O +N +H +CA
V_CB VAL CA CB CG1 CG2 HB
V_CG1 VAL CB CG1 HG11 HG12 HG13
V_CG2 VAL CB CG2 HG21 HG22 HG23

#ACE/NME: standard N-/C-terminal capping groups (acetyl / N-methylamide),
#used instead of NTER/CTER on a capped peptide. Verified against real
#connectivity (mdtraj on a real ACE/NME-capped peptide): ACE has no CA, so
#the next residue's own _PHI rule silently drops its "-CA" atom (missing,
#skipped per convention) -- the real -C/-O peptide-bond pairs it still
#finds are enough; nothing extra is needed on the ACE side beyond the two
#groups below. Same idea for NME on the C-terminal side.
ACE_C ACE CH3 C O +N
ACE_CH3 ACE C CH3 H1 H2 H3
NME_N NME -C N H C
NME_C NME N C H1 H2 H3
#Rigid-group definitions for DNA, springs-only articulated model: the
#backbone torsions alpha, beta, gamma, epsilon and zeta plus the glycosidic
#chi, as free hinges in an otherwise rigid mesh. The sugar pucker is NOT
#free -- and delta, which turns about a ring bond, is frozen with it; that
#is a deliberate choice, derived and justified in its own section below.
#No bonded energy of any kind -- every spring here carries pdb2spn's uniform
#--stiffness. Companion file for RNA: RNAAtomRigidGroups.rbody.
#
#Same columnar syntax and same reader as ProteinAtomRigidGroups.rbody: a
#.rbody line creates every pairwise spring between its atoms and leaves the
#particles distinct -- nothing is reduced or merged. Adjacent groups overlap
#on exactly the 2 atoms of a hinge axis, and that overlap alone gives
#"2 anchors, 1 free rotation".
#
#ATOM-NAME SPELLINGS ARE LISTED IN PAIRS THROUGHOUT, and that is load-bearing
#rather than cosmetic. The phosphate oxygens are O1P/O2P in amber99sb.xml and
#OP1/OP2 in PDB v3 and in AMBER's own DNA.OL15/RNA.OL3 -- the very files the
#bonded parameters come from. Same for the sugar hydrogens (H5'1 vs H5', ...).
#A group naming only one spelling silently matches nothing on a file written
#in the other: no error, just missing springs. Both are listed; the reader
#skips whichever atom is absent.
#
#CHARMM-style +/- prefix: "+X"/"-X" is atom X of the next/previous residue in
#the chain. A rule's resname is always its own (unprefixed) residue.
#
#DOUBLE STRAND -- read this before expecting base pairing. This format has no
#notion of chain: +/- walk the sequence, and there is no way to name "the
#paired base on the other strand". Nothing here holds the two strands
#together, and nothing here can. Both strands get these same per-residue
#rules independently, which is correct -- their rigidity is identical -- but
#the pairing itself must come from the hydrogen-bond mechanism (biospring's
#hbond.* settings and a .hbond donor/acceptor table), not from a rigid-body
#rule. A duplex built with this file and no hbond term will separate.
#
#Naming <ResidueCode>_<Part>, one group per backbone/sugar atom, walking 5'
#to 3'. Each group holds its own atom, its two neighbours along the chain or
#ring, and its hydrogens/substituents; consecutive groups then share exactly
#the 2 atoms of one rotatable bond. The seven free torsions come out as:
#   _P  /_O5  share {P, O5'}    -> alpha  (O3'(i-1)-P-O5'-C5')
#   _O5 /_C5  share {O5', C5'}  -> beta   (P-O5'-C5'-C4')
#   _C5 /_C4  share {C5', C4'}  -> gamma  (O5'-C5'-C4'-C3')
#   _C4 /_C3  share {C4', C3'}  -> delta  (C5'-C4'-C3'-O3')
#   _C3 /_O3  share {C3', O3'}  -> epsilon(C4'-C3'-O3'-P(i+1))
#   _O3 /next _P share {O3', P} -> zeta   (C3'-O3'-P(i+1)-O5'(i+1))
#   _C1 /_BASE share {C1', N9 or N1} -> chi (the glycosidic bond)
#
#THE SUGAR PUCKER IS FROZEN in this file, at whatever value the input
#structure carries. That is a deliberate choice, and it needs stating
#plainly because it is the one property a nucleic-acid model is judged on.
#
#The furanose is covered by five overlapping per-vertex groups (_C4, _O4,
#_C1, _C2, _C3), the same construction ProteinAtomRigidGroups.rbody uses for
#proline's pyrrolidine -- but that construction does NOT free a
#five-membered ring, and measuring it says so: all 10 ring pairs end up
#spring-connected. The reason is geometric and has no way around it. In a
#5-cycle every pair of atoms is at graph distance <= 2, so a group holding
#"vertex + its 2 ring neighbours" also creates the 1-3 spring between those
#two neighbours; summed over the five vertices that covers every pair.
#Fixing bond lengths AND valence angles is enough to rigidify a 5-ring.
#(Worth knowing: proline in the protein file has exactly the same property,
#10/10 -- its ring does not pucker either, whatever earlier notes claimed.)
#
#Freeing the pucker therefore requires dropping some of those 1-3 pairs, and
#in an energy-free model nothing would then hold the ring's angles: it would
#flatten or collapse. So the sugar stays rigid here, exactly as omega stays
#rigid in ProteinAtomRigidGroups.rbody -- a cis input stays cis, a C2'-endo
#input stays C2'-endo. Restoring a genuine, bimodal, correctly-populated
#pucker is the job of the DNA bonded parameters, and it is the single most
#important thing to validate when they land.
#
#Note delta rotates about C4'-C3', a RING bond, so it is not an independent
#hinge at all: it is a readout of the pucker (~84 deg in C3'-endo, ~144 deg
#in C2'-endo). With the ring rigid, delta is frozen with it -- the _C4/_C3
#overlap is still exactly 2 atoms, but the ring closure holds it. The other
#six torsions are genuinely free.
#
#Why the pucker matters at all: C2'-endo spaces the phosphates ~7 A along
#the strand (B-DNA, wide helix), C3'-endo ~5.9 A (A-DNA and RNA, compact).
#It is the lever translating a local sugar state into the global helix.
#
#THE BASE IS ONE RIGID CLIQUE here, deliberately, unlike the aromatic rings
#in ProteinAtomRigidGroups.rbody. Those were split per vertex only because
#the bonded force field supplies both their ring torsions and their
#PLANARITY impropers; freeing a 6-ring without those lets it fold ("boat")
#with no angle change, since its para pairs are 1-4. This file is the
#no-energy model, so a split base would simply collapse. Split it later, at
#the same time as the DNA bonded parameters land, not before.
#
#Terminal residues: a 5' end (DA5/DC5/DG5/DT5) has no P/O1P/O2P and carries
#H5T instead; a 3' end (DA3/...) carries H3T. Groups that reference a missing
#atom -- or a missing -O3'/+P neighbour at either chain end -- are skipped
#rather than failing, same tolerance as the protein file's first/last
#residue. The 5'/3' variant resnames are listed alongside the internal ones
#so a PDB using either spelling is covered.

#Adenine ------------------------------------------------------------------
A_P    DA  -O3' P O1P OP1 O2P OP2 O5'
A_O5   DA  P O5' C5'
A_C5   DA  O5' C5' H5'1 H5' H5'2 H5'' C4'
A_C4   DA  C5' C4' H4' O4' C3'
A_O4   DA  C4' O4' C1'
A_C1   DA  O4' C1' H1' C2' N9
A_C2   DA  C1' C2' H2'1 H2' H2'2 H2'' C3'
A_C3   DA  C2' C3' H3' C4' O3'
A_O3   DA  C3' O3' +P
A_BASE DA  C1' N9 C8 H8 N7 C5 C6 N6 H61 H62 N1 C2 H2 N3 C4
A_5TER DA  H5T O5' C5'
A_3TER DA  C3' O3' H3T

#Cytosine -----------------------------------------------------------------
C_P    DC  -O3' P O1P OP1 O2P OP2 O5'
C_O5   DC  P O5' C5'
C_C5   DC  O5' C5' H5'1 H5' H5'2 H5'' C4'
C_C4   DC  C5' C4' H4' O4' C3'
C_O4   DC  C4' O4' C1'
C_C1   DC  O4' C1' H1' C2' N1
C_C2   DC  C1' C2' H2'1 H2' H2'2 H2'' C3'
C_C3   DC  C2' C3' H3' C4' O3'
C_O3   DC  C3' O3' +P
C_BASE DC  C1' N1 C6 H6 C5 H5 C4 N4 H41 H42 N3 C2 O2
C_5TER DC  H5T O5' C5'
C_3TER DC  C3' O3' H3T

#Guanine ------------------------------------------------------------------
G_P    DG  -O3' P O1P OP1 O2P OP2 O5'
G_O5   DG  P O5' C5'
G_C5   DG  O5' C5' H5'1 H5' H5'2 H5'' C4'
G_C4   DG  C5' C4' H4' O4' C3'
G_O4   DG  C4' O4' C1'
G_C1   DG  O4' C1' H1' C2' N9
G_C2   DG  C1' C2' H2'1 H2' H2'2 H2'' C3'
G_C3   DG  C2' C3' H3' C4' O3'
G_O3   DG  C3' O3' +P
G_BASE DG  C1' N9 C8 H8 N7 C5 C6 O6 N1 H1 C2 N2 H21 H22 N3 C4
G_5TER DG  H5T O5' C5'
G_3TER DG  C3' O3' H3T

#Thymine ------------------------------------------------------------------
#C7 is the 5-methyl. Its three hydrogens sit inside the rigid base clique
#here, so the methyl does not spin in the no-energy model; give it its own
#{C5, C7, H71, H72, H73} group when the DNA torsion parameters land, exactly
#as ProteinAtomRigidGroups.rbody does for Ala/Val/Leu methyls.
T_P    DT  -O3' P O1P OP1 O2P OP2 O5'
T_O5   DT  P O5' C5'
T_C5   DT  O5' C5' H5'1 H5' H5'2 H5'' C4'
T_C4   DT  C5' C4' H4' O4' C3'
T_O4   DT  C4' O4' C1'
T_C1   DT  O4' C1' H1' C2' N1
